From: MeowArchMobile
Subject: [PATCH] sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp: stat through the fixed-size struct

mingw-w64 14 (as distros package it) declares wstat() in <sys/stat.h> as an
inline redirect to _wstat64(), whose second parameter is `struct _stat64*`.
Upstream passes `&st`, a `struct adb_stat` deriving from `struct stat`, and
that only compiles when the toolchain makes `stat` an alias for the
fixed-size struct — Google's prebuilt mingw does, distro mingw-w64 does not:

    error: cannot convert 'adb_stat*' to '_stat64*'
       56 |     int result = wstat(path_wide.c_str(), &st);

So stat into the fixed-size struct the function actually takes and copy the
result into the caller's, with a static_assert pinning the layout
equivalence that `struct adb_stat : public stat` already depends on. This
compiles on both flavours.

The other half of this bug was invisible until that one was fixed:
_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 has to reach EVERY translation unit. mingw picks both
the struct layout and the wstat entry point from it — sizeof(struct stat) is
48 with st_size at offset 20 without it, 56 with st_size at 24 with it.
sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp (compiled in adb_sysdeps) was getting 48 while
client/file_sync_client.cpp (compiled in the adb target, which inherits the
flag from ziparchive_static's PUBLIC definitions) was reading 56, so a
directory push took st_size from the wrong offset, resized a Block to the
resulting garbage and died:

    terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::bad_alloc'

while single-file pushes worked, because nothing stats a local directory on
that path. cmake/win32.cmake now sets the flag globally, as soong's
windowsCflags does.

diff --git a/sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp b/sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp
index 844c1ce..af1cad5 100644
--- a/sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp
+++ b/sysdeps/win32/stat.cpp
@@ -31,11 +31,14 @@ int adb_stat(const char* path, struct adb_stat* s) {
 #if defined(_FILE_OFFSET_BITS) && (_FILE_OFFSET_BITS == 64)
 #ifdef _USE_32BIT_TIME_T
 #define wstat _wstat32i64
+#define ADB_WSTAT_STRUCT struct _stat32i64
 #else
 #define wstat _wstat64
+#define ADB_WSTAT_STRUCT struct _stat64
 #endif
 #else
 // <sys/stat.h> has a function prototype for wstat() that should be available.
+#define ADB_WSTAT_STRUCT struct adb_stat
 #endif
 
     std::wstring path_wide;
@@ -52,8 +55,34 @@ int adb_stat(const char* path, struct adb_stat* s) {
         expected_directory = true;
     }
 
+    // Stat into the fixed-size struct wstat() actually takes, then copy into the
+    // caller's. Upstream passed `&st` (a `struct adb_stat`, which derives from
+    // `struct stat`) straight to wstat(), which only compiles when the
+    // toolchain makes `stat` an alias for that fixed-size struct: Google's
+    // prebuilt mingw does, but mingw-w64 as packaged by distros does not — there
+    // `struct stat` is its own struct and <sys/stat.h> declares wstat() as an
+    // inline redirect to _wstat64(), whose second parameter is `struct _stat64*`,
+    // so `adb_stat*` does not convert ("cannot convert 'adb_stat*' to
+    // '_stat64*'"). The copy is safe on either flavour: the assert below pins
+    // the layout equivalence that `struct adb_stat : public stat` in
+    // sysdeps/stat.h already depends on.
+    //
+    // Getting here at all requires _FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 to be set for EVERY
+    // translation unit, not just some. mingw picks both the struct layout and
+    // the wstat entry point from it (48 bytes with st_size at 20 without, 56
+    // with st_size at 24 with), so a writer compiled one way and a reader the
+    // other silently disagree about where st_size is — which is how a win32
+    // directory push read a garbage size out of local_build_list()'s lstat and
+    // died in Block::resize() with std::bad_alloc, while single-file pushes
+    // (which never stat a local directory) worked.
     struct adb_stat st;
-    int result = wstat(path_wide.c_str(), &st);
+    ADB_WSTAT_STRUCT wst;
+    static_assert(sizeof(wst) == sizeof(st),
+                  "adb_stat and the fixed-size wstat struct must be layout-compatible");
+    int result = wstat(path_wide.c_str(), &wst);
+    if (result == 0) {
+        memcpy(&st, &wst, sizeof(st));
+    }
     if (result == 0 && expected_directory) {
         if (!S_ISDIR(st.st_mode)) {
             errno = ENOTDIR;
